/**
@page TOPP_NASE_Rescoring Percolator rescoring in NASE

@brief Semi-supervised rescoring of the best hit per spectrum with the in-process Percolator.

@p rescoring:enable rescores NucleicAcidSearchEngine's spectrum matches with Percolator::rescore. It is off by
default and requires decoy sequences (@p fdr:decoy_pattern). The search, the candidate methods and the native
scores are unchanged; only the score that the q-values are calculated on changes.

@warning Experimental: the rescored q-values can understate the FDR when an analyte produces several spectra (see
@ref nase_rescoring_fdr). Check them with an entrapment experiment, and prefer shuffled decoys.

@code
NucleicAcidSearchEngine -in spectra.mzML -database targets_decoys.fasta \
  -out results.mzTab -fdr:decoy_pattern DECOY_ \
  -rescoring:enable -rescoring:out_features features.tsv
@endcode

@section nase_rescoring_rows What is rescored

Percolator gets one row per spectrum: its best hit by the native score. The other hits retained by the search
(@p rescoring:top_hits, default 2, at least @p report:top_hits) only describe how far ahead that hit is
(@p delta_next, @p delta_best). This is the usual Percolator input of search engines, so Percolator's PEPs and
NASE's q-values refer to the same competition: the best hit of each spectrum. Consequently:
- the order of the hits of a spectrum stays the native order; a runner-up is never promoted,
- hits tied with the best native score share its Percolator score and PEP,
- with @p report:top_hits above 1, lower-ranked hits get no Percolator score and therefore no q-value
  (with @p fdr:cutoff below 1, they are removed),
- the q-values count each spectrum once (its best hit), whatever @p report:top_hits is.

Runner-ups are not rescored as rows of their own. Every RNA target has an isobaric reversed decoy, so most
spectra have a strong decoy runner-up. Without Percolator's target-decoy competition, its training q-values and
PEPs count these runner-ups as independent chances; with it, the losing hits are returned as score 0 / PEP 1
and cannot be told apart from real values.

@section nase_rescoring_features Features

The rows come from RNARescoringFeatures. Their columns depend only on the parameters (and on the charges of the
rescored hits), never on the label of a hit:
- @p native_score: hyperscore, MVH or route score
- @p delta_next: score minus the best lower or equal score of a hit with another sequence (other charges or
  isotopes of the same sequence are no competitors); @p delta_best: score minus the spectrum's best score
- @p abs_ppm_error, @p isotope_offset, one-hot @p charge_N (N = precursor charge), @p length, @p modifications, @p adduct
- @p frac_X (X = ion series): distinct matched ordinals of each @p fragment:ions series / (length - 1)
- @p matched_ions: matched (series, ordinal) pairs / (series * (length - 1))
- @p matched_intensity: intensity of the distinct matched peaks / total intensity of the scored spectrum
- @p longest_series: longest run of consecutive ordinals of one series / (length - 1)
- @p coverage: backbone positions explained by any series / (length - 1)

Optional groups: route evidence (@p scoring:method=route, whose route score is @p native_score; or
@p rescoring:route_features with hyperscore or MVH, which adds @p route_score: the route score of the hit's intact
model, without the support scaffolds of @p graph_routes), @p graph_tag_length (graph candidate methods except
@p graph_routes), modifications with diagnostic support (@p diagnostic:filter), and FLASH precursor
cosine and SNR (@p precursor:correct_with_flash).

@section nase_rescoring_output Output and fallback

The PSM section gets @p percolator:score (MS:1001492) and @p percolator:PEP (MS:1001493) next to the native
score; the PSM-level q-value is calculated on the Percolator score by the usual NASE FDR path.
@p rescoring:out_features writes the features, label and Percolator score, q-value and PEP of each rescored
spectrum.

NASE keeps the native score, with a warning, if fewer than @p rescoring:min_decoys spectra (default 100) have a
decoy as their best hit (or no target does), if Percolator's sanity check rejects the learned model, or if
Percolator (or the route evidence of @p rescoring:route_features) fails. The output is then that of a search without
rescoring. (For a rejected model, Percolator would
score with its best single feature, which can rank far worse than the native score.) @p rescoring:train_fdr
(default 0.01) sets the FDR at which Percolator selects positive training examples and checks the learned model; a
higher value, e.g. 0.05, can let hard searches train.

@section nase_rescoring_fdr FDR control

Percolator trains with three-fold cross-validation, and NASE puts every spectrum in a fold of its own. When one
analyte produces several spectra, some of them train the model that scores the others. Freestone, Noble and Keich
(2024, "Re-investigating the correctness of decoy-based false discovery rate control in proteomics tandem mass
spectrometry") showed that this lets Percolator's PSM-level q-values understate the FDR: repeated spectra of a
wrongly explained analyte pull each other up when the wrong match is a target and push each other down when it is a
decoy. Entrapment checks of NASE on HAP1 files with simplified settings showed the same pattern: accepted entrapment
matches clustered on a few repeated oligos, with estimated FDPs of about 7-13% at q <= 1% in one check and about 12%
at q <= 5% on one file in another; with the full HAP1 recipe the estimates stayed near nominal. Until rescoring groups
the spectra of one analyte (e.g. oligo-level training, or Percolator-RESET by Freestone, Käll, Noble and Keich), the
rescored q-values are experimental. Shuffled decoys (DecoyDatabase with @p type RNA and @p method shuffle) gave the
lower estimated FDP in these checks and are recommended over reversed ones.

Results are reproducible for the same @p rescoring:seed and input order; with 1 and 4 threads, the scores and q-values
were identical in our checks and only the PEPs differed slightly (core Percolator's PEP estimation). The tests use
synthetic data (one set on which Percolator trains a model, one on which it rejects it) and check the wiring, not
the model. Reversed RNA decoys share many fragment masses with their
targets; shuffled decoys separated better in the entrapment checks. Neither a
nominal q-value yield nor these tests show FDR control on real data; an entrapment experiment is the appropriate
check.
*/
